# Part One — The Paper Itself

## 1.1  Where Gender Studies sits in CSS

The CSS Main Examination carries ==1,200 marks==: six compulsory papers (==600 marks==) and optional papers worth another ==600 marks==, chosen from seven groups. Gender Studies is **subject code 31** in **Group V**, where a candidate chooses one subject of 100 marks from seven: Gender Studies, Environmental Sciences, Agriculture and Forestry, Botany, Zoology, English Literature and Urdu Literature.

::: table | Gender Studies in the CSS scheme.
@widths 4,10
Item | The facts
Paper | Gender Studies — one paper of 100 marks (Group V, code 31)
Structure | Part I: 20 MCQs (20 marks). Part II: seven questions, attempt any four (80 marks)
Time | Three hours in all: Part I has a maximum of 30 minutes; Part II has the remaining two and a half hours
Pass mark | 33 marks in an optional paper (and 50% aggregate in the written examination overall)
First set | CE-2016 — the subject entered the revised scheme "for CE-2016 and onwards"; there was no Gender Studies paper in CE-2015
Record in this set | Eleven papers, CE-2016 to CE-2026; MCQ sheets recovered for seven years
:::

Why candidates choose it, and why it rewards them:

- **The syllabus is short and self-contained.** Nine heads, most of them with four to seven lines. A disciplined candidate can cover it thoroughly in three to four months alongside other papers.
- **It feeds the compulsory papers.** Gender is one of the most common themes in the English Essay paper ("Women's empowerment is the key to Pakistan's development"; "Gender equality — myth or reality?"). It overlaps with Current Affairs (the SDGs, climate, the Global Gender Gap), Pakistan Affairs (the Constitution, social problems) and Governance.
- **It overlaps with other optional subjects** — Sociology, Governance and Public Policies, Criminology, Psychology — so notes serve twice.
- **It rewards method.** Because most candidates write general, opinion-based answers, a candidate who defines terms, names theories, cites data and law, draws a diagram and applies it all to Pakistan stands out at once.

::: eye | The paper everyone thinks is "general knowledge"
Because gender is discussed on television every day, many candidates assume they can write the paper from common sense. The examiner therefore reads hundreds of answers that say "women are the backbone of society" and little else. The candidate who writes "Liberal feminists such as Betty Friedan argue… but socialist feminists such as Heidi Hartmann reply…", and then gives the Labour Force Survey figure, is in a different league. That gap is the whole strategy of these notes.
:::

## 1.2  The syllabus, word for word

This is the syllabus exactly as FPSC prints it ("for CE-2016 and onwards"), with the topic of these notes in which each head is taught. Two names are misspelt in the official syllabus — "Mallala Yousaf Zai" and "Shermin Ubaid Chinoy". Always write them correctly: **Malala Yousafzai** and **Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy**.

::: table | The official syllabus and where each head is taught.
@widths 4,8.5,1.5
Head | Syllabus lines | Topic
I. Introduction to Gender Studies | Introduction to Gender Studies · Difference between Gender and Women Studies · Multi-disciplinary nature of Gender Studies · Autonomy vs. Integration Debate in Gender Studies · Status of Gender Studies in Pakistan | T1
II. Social Construction of Gender | Historicizing Constructionism · Problematizing the category of "Sex": Queer Theory · Is "Sex" socially determined, too? · Masculinities and Femininity · Nature versus Culture: A Debate in Gender Development | T1
III. Feminist Theories and Practice | What is Feminism · Liberal Feminism · Radical Feminism · Marxist/Socialist Feminism · Psychoanalytical Feminism · Men's Feminism · Postmodern Feminism | T2
IV. Feminist Movements | Feminist Movements in the West: First Wave, Second Wave and Third Wave Feminism · United Nation Conferences on Women · Feminist Movements in Pakistan | T3
V. Gender and Development | Colonial and Capitalistic Perspectives of Gender · Gender Analysis of Development Theories: Modernization Theory, World System Theory, Dependency Theory, Structural Functionalism · Gender Approaches to Development: WID, WAD, GAD; Gender Critique of Structural Adjustment Policies (SAPs) · Globalization and Gender | T4
VI. Status of Women in Pakistan | Status of Women's health in Pakistan · Status of Women in Education · Women and Employment · Women and Law | T5
VII. Gender and Governance | Defining Governance · Suffragist Movement · Gender Issues in Women as Voters · Gender Issues in Women as Candidates · Gender Issues in Women as Representatives · Impact of Political Quota in Pakistan | T6
VIII. Gender Based Violence | Defining Gender Based Violence · Theories of Violence against Women · Structural and Direct Forms of Violence · Strategies to Eliminate Violence against Women | T7
IX. Case Studies of | Mukhtaran Mai · Malala Yousafzai · Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy | T7
:::

Four things about this syllabus matter for your preparation:

1. **It is older than the paper it produces.** Written in the mid-2010s, it says nothing about intersectionality, climate change, digital gender gaps or the fourth wave. The examiner of 2024–2026 asks about all of them. These notes therefore teach every syllabus line *and* the contemporary layer the examiner has added.
2. **Some lines are single words that hide whole debates.** "Women and Law" covers the Constitution, family law, criminal law, the Hudood Ordinances, the protective laws of 2004–2025, inheritance and property. "Men's Feminism" covers pro-feminist men, masculinity studies and the HeForShe campaign.
3. **Head IV says "First, Second and Third Wave".** The fourth wave is not named, but the examiner has asked about "the different waves" (2025) and "digital activism" (2026), so it is taught too.
4. **Head IX is three people.** Each case study is a doorway into a larger theme: Mukhtaran Mai into jirga justice and rape law; Malala into education, militancy and global advocacy; Sharmeen Obaid-Chinoy into acid violence, honour killing and the power of media to change law.

## 1.3  The reading list — which books actually help

FPSC lists twenty suggested readings. Some are standard textbooks; some are articles or reports that are hard to find. The table tells you honestly what each gives you. You do not need to read them cover to cover — the Knowledge Bases distil them — but knowing their names and arguments is itself evidence in an answer.

::: table | The official reading list, rated.
@widths 4.8,2.9,5,1.3
@font 9
Title | Author(s) | What it gives you | Use
*Theories of Women's Studies* | Gloria Bowles and Renate Duelli Klein (eds., 1983) | The founding collection; the introduction frames the **autonomy versus integration** debate | T1 ★
"Introduction: Theories of Women's Studies and the Autonomy/Integration Debate" | Bowles and Klein | The exact text of the debate the examiner has asked four times | T1 ★
"The State of the Discipline of Women's Studies in Pakistan" (in *Social Sciences in Pakistan*) | Rubina Saigol | The history and problems of the field in Pakistan | T1
"The State of Women's Studies in Pakistan" (*Journal of Asian Women's Studies*) | Farzana Bari | The centres of 1989, their achievements and weaknesses | T1
*Women's Law in Legal Education and Practice in Pakistan* | Farida Shaheed and Rubya Mehdi (eds., 1997) | Women and the legal system; family and criminal law | T5
*Locating the Self: Perspectives on Women and Multiple Identities* | Nighat Said Khan et al. (eds., 1998) | Identity, religion, ethnicity and class in women's lives — early intersectionality | T1, T2
*The Social Construction of Gender* | Judith Lorber | The classic account of gender as a social institution | T1 ★
"Queer Theory" (in *Gender Inequality*) | Judith Lorber | A clear short account of queer theory | T1
*Gender* (2nd ed., 2013) | Harriet Bradley | A modern textbook on theories of gender | T1, T2
*Women of Pakistan: Two Steps Forward, One Step Back?* | Khawar Mumtaz and Farida Shaheed (1987) | The standard history of the women's movement to the Zia era, by two WAF founders | T3 ★
*Feminism and its Relevance in South Asia* | Kamla Bhasin and Nighat Said Khan | What feminism means in South Asia; answers the "Western import" charge | T2, T3
*Feminist Thought* | Rosemarie Tong | The best single guide to the schools of feminism | T2 ★
*Reversed Realities: Gender Hierarchies in Development Thought* | Naila Kabeer (1994) | WID/WAD/GAD, empowerment, the household | T4 ★
*Gender Planning and Development* | Caroline Moser (1993) | Practical and strategic gender needs; the triple role; gender planning | T4 ★
*Electoral Politics: Making Quotas Work for Women* | Homa Hoodfar and Mona Tajali (2011) | Quotas in Muslim-majority countries, including Pakistan | T6 ★
*The Gender Face of Asian Politics* | Aazar Ayaz and Andrea Fleschenberg (eds., 2009) | Women politicians in Asia, dynasties and quotas | T6
*Performance Assessment of Women Parliamentarians in Pakistan* | Farzana Bari (2002) | How women legislators performed after the quota | T6
*Baseline Report: Women's Participation in Political and Public Life* | Shahla Zia and Farzana Bari (1999) | The situation before the 2002 quota | T6
*Freedom from Violence: Women's Strategies from Around the World* | Margaret Schuler (ed., 1992) | Strategies to end violence against women | T7
*State of Human Rights* (annual report) | Human Rights Commission of Pakistan | Yearly data on violence, honour killing, law and justice | T5, T7 ★
:::

### Beyond the list — the sources these notes also draw on

The examiner rewards up-to-date evidence. The notes therefore draw on these regularly updated sources, and the Fact Book records the latest figure from each:

- **World Economic Forum**, *Global Gender Gap Report* (annual; 2026 edition) — asked by name in 2026.
- **Pakistan Bureau of Statistics** — Census 2023; Labour Force Survey 2024–25; PSLM surveys.
- **National Institute of Population Studies** — Pakistan Demographic and Health Survey; Pakistan Maternal Mortality Survey (2019).
- **Election Commission of Pakistan** — electoral rolls by gender; election results.
- **National Commission on the Status of Women (NCSW)**; the provincial women's commissions.
- **UN Women**, **UNDP** (Human Development Report; Gender Inequality Index), **WHO**, **UNICEF**, **UNFPA**.
- **GSMA**, *Mobile Gender Gap Report* — the source of the digital-gap figures asked in 2026.
- **Human Rights Commission of Pakistan**; **Sustainable Social Development Organisation**; **Digital Rights Foundation** — data on violence and online harassment.

## 1.4  How the paper is built

Every Gender Studies paper since 2016 has had the same shape. Knowing it in advance removes surprise on the day.

::: fig flow | The shape of the paper: two parts, one answer book for Part II.
@per 4
Part I · MCQs: 20 questions, 1 mark each, 20 marks; maximum 30 minutes; answered on the separate sheet
Part II · Descriptive: Q.2 to Q.8 — seven questions of 20 marks
Choose four: 4 × 20 = 80 marks in two and a half hours
Q.8 is often short notes: two notes of 10 marks (in 2017, 2018, 2019, 2024, 2026)
:::

### Part I — the objective questions (MCQs)

Twenty multiple-choice questions, each with four options, one mark each. They test names (who wrote *The Second Sex*?), dates (when did Pakistan accede to CEDAW?), terms (what is androgyny?), laws (when was the workplace harassment law passed?) and, increasingly, concepts (what does Butler mean by performativity?). Part Three shows that the MCQ paper has moved sharply from Pakistan facts towards feminist theory since 2023.

::: trap | The defective MCQ
Several recovered MCQs have no fully correct option, or a published key that is doubtful (for example CSS 2025 Q3 and Q14; several 2026 items whose correct answer was "None of these"). Do not panic when no option looks perfect. Choose the best answer, move on, and do not waste minutes arguing with the paper.
:::

### Part II — the descriptive questions

Seven questions numbered 2 to 8 (Question 1 is the MCQ part). Attempt any four; all carry equal marks. The questions come in a small number of shapes, analysed in §3.9: the definition-and-difference question, the explain-a-theory question, the critically-evaluate question, the Pakistan-applied question, the case-study question and the short notes. Since 2024 a new shape has appeared: the **long, loaded question** that names a report, a law or a statistic and asks you to apply a theory to it (for example 2026 Q2, Q4 and Q5).

### The 2015 exception

The past-paper archive in this folder includes a 2015 page for completeness. Gender Studies first appears in the revised scheme "for CE-2016 and onwards", so there was no CE-2015 paper. The record in these notes therefore runs from ==2016 to 2026==: eleven papers.

## 1.5  The marks and the minutes

Three hours pass quickly. Plan them before the day, and keep to the plan.

::: table | A working time plan for the three hours.
@widths 3,2,9
Stage | Minutes | What you do
Part I (MCQs) | 20–25 | Answer all twenty. Mark doubtful ones, return to them, never leave a blank. Hand in the sheet on time.
Choosing | 8–10 | Read all seven questions twice. Tick the four you can answer best — prefer questions where you have theory *and* Pakistan evidence. Note the command words.
Answer maps | 4 × 3 | For each chosen question, draw a quick map in the margin or on the rough sheet: introduction, numbered headings, evidence for each, diagram, conclusion.
Writing | 4 × 30 | About 30 minutes per answer: roughly six to eight handwritten pages (about 1,000–1,300 words).
Review | 5–8 | Underline key terms, check numbers and names, number your headings clearly.
:::

How are the 20 marks of a descriptive answer earned? FPSC does not publish a marking scheme. The table below is a practical guide built from what examiners' reports and experienced markers say they reward. Use it to check your practice answers.

::: table | Where the 20 marks come from (a working guide, not an official scheme).
@widths 4,1.5,8.5
Element | Marks | What earns them
Introduction | 2 | Definitions of the key terms; context; a one-sentence thesis that answers the question.
Structure | 3 | Numbered headings taken from the question's words; logical order; every limb of the question answered.
Content | 8 | Correct, relevant knowledge: theories, arguments, history, analysis.
Evidence | 4 | Named thinkers, dated data from named sources, laws, reports and cases — inside the points.
Critical and Pakistan angle | 2 | Counter-arguments, evaluation, application to Pakistan.
Conclusion | 1 | A judgement and a way forward, not a repetition.
:::

## 1.6  Why candidates fail — and how to be in the other group

Candidates rarely fail Gender Studies because they know nothing. They fail, or score in the thirties and low forties, for predictable reasons:

1. **Opinion instead of analysis.** "Women are oppressed in our society due to illiteracy and male dominance" repeated for six pages. The cure: name theories and thinkers, and explain causes.
2. **No definitions.** Writing about "feminism" or "gender mainstreaming" without ever saying what it means. The cure: define every key term in your introduction.
3. **Answering the topic, not the question.** Asked to "evaluate the impact of the quota", the candidate writes the history of women in politics. The cure: take your headings from the question's own words (Part Five).
4. **One limb only.** Many questions have two or three parts ("Differentiate… and discuss…"). Half-answered questions cap at half marks. The cure: the answer map, which puts each limb on the page before you write.
5. **Western theory with no Pakistan.** Or Pakistani complaints with no theory. The cure: every heading links the two.
6. **Doubtful facts.** "Pakistan is the worst country for women" (it is not a ranking any report makes in those words), "50% of women are illiterate" (out of date). The cure: the Fact Book, with sources and years.
7. **Shouting.** Attacks on the West, on religion, on men or on feminists. The cure: the balanced view (§0.9).
8. **Poor time control.** A brilliant first answer and a half-page fourth. The cure: the time plan above, practised on the Prediction Papers.

## 1.7  The eleven papers at a glance

The table shows where every question of every paper came from, using the seven topics of these notes (explained in Part Two). Read along a row to see the balance of a single paper; read down the columns and you will see that no topic is ever safe to skip.

@py papers_glance

Three patterns are visible at once:

- **Almost every paper visits five topics or more.** Three papers (2018, 2019, 2024) touched all seven; only 2021 visited as few as four. A candidate who skips two topics risks finding only three answerable questions. Selective study is dangerous.
- **T1 (the discipline and social construction) dominated the early papers** — two questions in most years from 2016 to 2021, and three in 2023 — but then almost vanished: one question in 2024 (a short note), none in 2025, one in 2026. Its questions have become sharper and more theoretical (queer theory and the transgender law in 2026).
- **T4 (gender and development) is present in every single paper from 2016 to 2026.** It is the one topic you can be sure of meeting.
